      <h2>5. The Contiguous Urban Area (8-Kilometer Dependency) Rule</h2>
      <p>
        One of the most litigated and vital provisions in Central Civil Services (Allowance) Rules is the <strong>Contiguous Area Rule</strong>, which addresses central establishments located outside municipal city limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        Under Ministry of Finance regulations:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The 8-Kilometer Perimeter:</strong> If a central government office, defence depot, atomic facility, or research laboratory is situated outside the municipal limits of a qualifying Class X or Class Y city, but within a distance of <strong>8 kilometers from the periphery</strong> of the municipal boundary, employees may claim the higher city's HRA rate.</li>
        <li><strong>The Dependency Certificate:</strong> For the higher rate to be sanctioned, the administrative ministry must obtain a formal dependency certificate from the District Collector or Municipal Commissioner confirming that:
          <ol>
            <li>The suburban enclave forms a continuous urban extension with unbroken residential and commercial infrastructure.</li>
            <li>Employees stationed at the establishment are compelled to reside inside the main qualifying city due to a lack of civic housing, schools, and medical facilities within the immediate suburban vicinity.</li>
          </ol>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. The Minimum Floor Protection Guarantee</h2>
      <p>
        To protect entry-level operational personnel (such as Multi-Tasking Staff and Lower Division Clerks in Levels 1 and 2) stationed in high-cost metropolitan centers, the Central Government mandates a statutory <strong>HRA Minimum Floor Limit</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Under this rule, if the mathematical calculation of an employee's basic pay multiplied by the city percentage yields an amount lower than the statutory floor, the Accounts Office must pay the guaranteed floor amount:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>7th CPC Floor Limits:</strong> Fixed at <strong>₹5,400</strong> (Class X), <strong>₹3,600</strong> (Class Y), and <strong>₹1,800</strong> (Class Z).</li>
        <li><strong>8th CPC Projections:</strong> Applying the standard 2.25x to 2.86x fitment multipliers, these guaranteed minimum floors will adjust upwards to approximately <strong>₹12,150 – ₹15,444</strong> (Class X), <strong>₹8,100 – ₹10,296</strong> (Class Y), and <strong>₹4,050 – ₹5,148</strong> (Class Z).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>7. Dual-Spouse Employment & Government Quarters Forfeiture</h2>
      <p>
        Central Civil Services rules enforce strict restrictions to prevent dual housing disbursements within a single household:
      </p>
      <h3>1. Forfeiture on Government Quarters (GPRA)</h3>
      <p>
        When an employee accepts government accommodation (General Pool Residential Accommodation - GPRA, railway quarters, or defence cantonment housing), they forfeit <strong>100% of their monthly HRA</strong> from the physical date of taking possession. In addition, a nominal standard monthly license fee is deducted from their salary.
      </p>
      <h3>2. Spouses Stationed at the Same Duty Station</h3>
      <p>
        If both husband and wife are public servants (central government, state government, or public sector undertakings) stationed at the same headquarters:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One Spouse in Quarters:</strong> If either spouse occupies government-allotted quarters, the other spouse is <strong>strictly barred</strong> from drawing HRA, even if they claim to maintain separate residential dwellings.</li>
        <li><strong>Living in Private Rented Housing:</strong> If both spouses reside in private rented accommodation, only one spouse is entitled to draw House Rent Allowance on their pay slip.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>3. Spouses Stationed at Different Duty Stations</h3>
      <p>
        If a married couple is stationed at separate, geographically distinct duty stations (e.g., the husband in Delhi and the wife in Bengaluru), both employees are independently entitled to draw HRA applicable to their respective city classifications, provided neither occupies government quarters at their duty post.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Tax Implications: Exemption under Section 10(13A)</h2>
      <p>
        The taxability of House Rent Allowance depends on the employee's tax regime selection and housing arrangements:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Under the Old Tax Regime:</strong> Employees residing in private rented housing can claim substantial tax exemptions under <strong>Section 10(13A)</strong> read with Rule 2A. The exempt amount is the lowest of:
          <ol>
            <li>Total actual HRA received during the financial year.</li>
            <li>Actual rent paid minus 10% of basic salary (including Dearness Allowance).</li>
            <li>50% of basic salary for metro cities (Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai) or 40% for all other locations.</li>
          </ol>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Under the New Tax Regime:</strong> HRA exemptions under Section 10(13A) are <strong>completely disallowed</strong>. Employees opting for the default New Tax Regime are taxed on their full gross HRA earnings.</li>
      </ul>

